  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively establish that high-temperature exposure can produce non-sluggish, element-selective diffusion, surface-directed depletion, segregation, and evaporation in multicomponent high-entropy alloys, and that kinetic effects can override nominal high-entropy stabilization. R7, R11, and R15 are particularly relevant to preferential loss or outward transport of volatile or fast-diffusing elements, while R3 supplies a general surface-energy consideration. However, none describes AuCoCuNiPt nanoparticles, vacuum heating of initially twinned/icosahedral particles, low-surface-energy-driven Au/Cu surface segregation, or reshaping-enhanced local diffusion. Thus the literature points in the right direction, but combining these ideas into the specific nanoparticle intervention and mechanism would require a substantial new insight."
